// Chapter 5: Privacy and Security (40 questions)
const chapter5Questions: QuizQuestion[] = [
  makeQuestion(1, "A fitness app collects users' location, heart rate, and sleep data, then sells aggregated profiles to third-party advertisers without clear user consent. Which core concept is most directly implicated?", ["Informational privacy — an individual's right to control their own personal data", "Freedom of the press", "Trademark protection", "Fair pricing"], 0, "Informational privacy — an individual's right to control their own personal data."),
  makeQuestion(2, "Which best captures the modern understanding of \"privacy\" in the information age, beyond the classic \"right to be let alone\"?", ["The right to control the collection, use, and disclosure of one's personal information", "The right to unlimited free speech in all contexts", "The right to own all forms of intellectual property", "The right to a minimum wage"], 0, "The right to control the collection, use, and disclosure of one's personal information."),
  makeQuestion(3, "A landlord installs hidden cameras inside tenants' private living spaces without their knowledge, claiming it is for \"security purposes.\" This most clearly violates:", ["The tenants' reasonable expectation of privacy in their own homes", "A copyright interest in the apartment's design", "A trademark right", "A just wage principle"], 0, "The tenants' reasonable expectation of privacy in their own homes."),
  makeQuestion(4, "An employer reads employees' personal, non-work social media accounts using credentials they were never given, purely out of curiosity about their off-duty lives. Which privacy concept is most clearly at stake?", ["Privacy extends to aspects of a person's life outside their professional role, absent a legitimate, disclosed business reason", "Employers automatically own all information about their employees", "Privacy applies only to financial information, not social media", "There is no reasonable privacy expectation once someone is employed"], 0, "Privacy extends to aspects of a person's life outside their professional role, absent a legitimate, disclosed business reason."),
  makeQuestion(5, "A company collects far more personal data from users than necessary to provide its service, arguing that data \"might be useful someday.\" Which privacy principle does this most violate?", ["Data minimization", "Freedom of expression", "Fair use", "Trademark registration"], 0, "Data minimization."),
  makeQuestion(6, "An individual voluntarily posts personal information on a public social media profile. Does this eliminate all reasonable privacy expectations regarding that information?", ["Not entirely; secondary uses (e.g., large-scale scraping, profiling, or repurposing beyond the original context) can still raise privacy concerns even for public posts", "Yes, anything ever posted publicly loses all privacy protection forever, in every context", "Yes, but only if the person has fewer than 100 followers", "No, public posts always retain the exact same protection as private messages"], 0, "Not entirely; secondary uses (e.g., large-scale scraping, profiling, or repurposing beyond the original context) can still raise privacy concerns even for public posts."),
  makeQuestion(7, "A hospital shares a patient's medical records with a research team without explicit consent, but properly anonymizes the data so no individual can be identified. How does this affect the privacy analysis?", ["Effective anonymization significantly reduces (though does not always eliminate) privacy concerns, compared to sharing identifiable data", "Anonymization has no effect at all on privacy analysis", "Anonymized data is always illegal to use regardless of purpose", "Anonymization automatically makes any use illegal"], 0, "Effective anonymization significantly reduces (though does not always eliminate) privacy concerns, compared to sharing identifiable data."),
  makeQuestion(8, "A company profiles users' browsing habits across many unrelated sites to build detailed psychological and behavioral profiles used for manipulation, not just relevant ads. Compared to basic, transparent ad targeting, this raises heightened concern because:", ["It aggregates and infers deeply personal information beyond what any single disclosed data point would reveal, often without meaningful consent", "There is no meaningful difference between the two practices", "Both practices are equally acceptable regardless of scope", "Aggregation always makes data less sensitive, not more"], 0, "It aggregates and infers deeply personal information beyond what any single disclosed data point would reveal, often without meaningful consent."),
  makeQuestion(9, "Employees frequently share confidential client information informally because \"everyone already knows anyway,\" undermining the organization's confidentiality culture. What is the deeper value at risk here?", ["Respect for privacy as a professional norm, not merely a rule to be selectively enforced", "Trademark dilution", "Copyright infringement", "A labor strike concern"], 0, "Respect for privacy as a professional norm, not merely a rule to be selectively enforced."),
  makeQuestion(10, "Why do many ethicists argue that privacy is not just a personal preference, but has broader social value?", ["Privacy enables autonomy, intimacy, and freedom from undue social or governmental control, benefiting society as a whole, not just individuals", "Privacy benefits only wealthy individuals with something to hide", "Privacy has no broader social value beyond individual comfort", "Privacy is valuable only in authoritarian societies"], 0, "Privacy enables autonomy, intimacy, and freedom from undue social or governmental control, benefiting society as a whole, not just individuals."),
  makeQuestion(11, "A company argues, \"If you have nothing to hide, you have nothing to fear from surveillance.\" What is a common ethical counterargument to this reasoning?", ["Privacy protects more than wrongdoing — it protects autonomy, dignity, and the ability to make choices free from constant monitoring and judgment", "There is no valid counterargument; the statement is always correct", "Privacy only matters for criminals", "Surveillance has never had any documented negative effects on innocent people"], 0, "Privacy protects more than wrongdoing — it protects autonomy, dignity, and the ability to make choices free from constant monitoring and judgment."),
  makeQuestion(12, "A researcher argues that privacy is valuable because it allows people to develop personal relationships, explore ideas, and make decisions without the pressure of constant observation. This best reflects privacy's role in supporting:", ["Individual autonomy and personal development", "Corporate profit maximization", "Trademark protection", "Labor productivity metrics alone"], 0, "Individual autonomy and personal development."),
  makeQuestion(13, "A workplace requires employees to wear devices that continuously track their exact location and biometric stress levels throughout the entire workday, even during breaks. Beyond productivity concerns, what deeper value does excessive monitoring threaten?", ["Human dignity and autonomy, which can be eroded by pervasive, unrelenting surveillance", "Trademark rights", "Copyright protection of the tracking software", "Fair pricing of the devices"], 0, "Human dignity and autonomy, which can be eroded by pervasive, unrelenting surveillance."),
  makeQuestion(14, "A company argues employee productivity monitoring should have no limits since \"the employer owns the work hours.\" What is a reasonable ethical counterpoint?", ["Even during work hours, employees retain some baseline dignity and privacy interests that should be balanced against legitimate monitoring needs", "Employers are legally and ethically entitled to unlimited surveillance with no boundaries whatsoever", "Employees fully forfeit all privacy the moment they accept a job offer", "Monitoring concerns are purely a legal matter with no ethical dimension"], 0, "Even during work hours, employees retain some baseline dignity and privacy interests that should be balanced against legitimate monitoring needs."),
  makeQuestion(15, "Which best explains why privacy is considered foundational to intimate relationships and personal identity formation?", ["Privacy provides the space needed to selectively share oneself with trusted others, which is essential for trust, intimacy, and authentic self-expression", "Privacy prevents all forms of human connection", "Privacy is unrelated to psychological or relational well-being", "Privacy is only relevant to financial matters"], 0, "Privacy provides the space needed to selectively share oneself with trusted others, which is essential for trust, intimacy, and authentic self-expression."),
  makeQuestion(16, "An authoritarian government requires all citizens' internet activity to be logged and reviewed by state agents, ostensibly for \"national security,\" without judicial oversight or narrow scope. Which democratic value is most directly threatened?", ["Privacy as a check against unchecked governmental power and potential abuse", "Trademark law", "Copyright law", "Labor law"], 0, "Privacy as a check against unchecked governmental power and potential abuse."),
  makeQuestion(17, "Why is privacy often described as essential to a functioning democracy, beyond individual comfort?", ["Privacy protects the ability to hold unpopular opinions, engage in political dissent, and vote freely without fear of surveillance-driven retaliation", "Privacy has no meaningful connection to democratic processes", "Democracies function better with unlimited government surveillance of all citizens", "Privacy is relevant only to economic, not political, freedoms"], 0, "Privacy protects the ability to hold unpopular opinions, engage in political dissent, and vote freely without fear of surveillance-driven retaliation."),
  makeQuestion(18, "A government agency conducts mass, warrantless surveillance of journalists and activists critical of its policies, citing vague security justifications. This scenario best illustrates the risk that:", ["Unchecked surveillance can chill free expression and dissent, both core pillars of democratic society", "Surveillance always strengthens democratic institutions with no downside", "Journalists have no legitimate privacy interest whatsoever", "This raises only copyright concerns, not democratic ones"], 0, "Unchecked surveillance can chill free expression and dissent, both core pillars of democratic society."),
  makeQuestion(19, "A newly elected government proposes requiring real-name registration and government monitoring for all social media activity, citing the need to combat misinformation. Civil liberties advocates raise concerns primarily because:", ["Such broad monitoring could chill legitimate political speech and disproportionately affect government critics, beyond its stated narrow purpose", "Misinformation is never a legitimate governmental concern", "There is no connection between anonymity and free political expression", "Social media has no relationship to democratic participation"], 0, "Such broad monitoring could chill legitimate political speech and disproportionately affect government critics, beyond its stated narrow purpose."),
  makeQuestion(20, "Which historical development is most closely associated with prompting modern privacy law frameworks (e.g., data protection regulations) in the latter half of the 20th century?", ["The rise of computerized databases enabling large-scale collection, storage, and cross-referencing of personal information", "The invention of the printing press", "The abolition of slavery", "The creation of the first stock exchange"], 0, "The rise of computerized databases enabling large-scale collection, storage, and cross-referencing of personal information."),
  makeQuestion(21, "Early privacy law scholarship, notably Warren and Brandeis's influential work, framed privacy primarily as:", ["\"The right to be let alone,\" originally developed partly in response to intrusive newspaper reporting", "A purely modern internet-era concept with no historical roots", "A concept that applies only to corporations, not individuals", "A right that exists only in criminal, not civil, law"], 0, "\"The right to be let alone,\" originally developed partly in response to intrusive newspaper reporting."),
  makeQuestion(22, "The Philippine Data Privacy Act of 2012 reflects a broader global historical trend of governments responding to which development?", ["The explosive growth of digital data collection and processing capabilities, requiring updated legal frameworks", "A decline in the amount of personal data being collected worldwide", "The elimination of the internet in most countries", "A global movement to abolish all forms of privacy law"], 0, "The explosive growth of digital data collection and processing capabilities, requiring updated legal frameworks."),
  makeQuestion(23, "Comparing early 20th-century privacy law (focused on physical intrusion, like trespass or unauthorized photography) to today's data privacy laws, what is the most significant shift?", ["A shift toward regulating the collection, processing, and use of intangible personal data, not just physical intrusion into private spaces", "A complete abandonment of privacy law protections", "A shift toward protecting only corporations' privacy, not individuals'", "No meaningful shift has occurred at all"], 0, "A shift toward regulating the collection, processing, and use of intangible personal data, not just physical intrusion into private spaces."),
  makeQuestion(24, "An international framework (e.g., the EU's data protection principles) has significantly influenced privacy law development in many countries, including aspects reflected in the Philippines' own Data Privacy Act. This illustrates:", ["Privacy law has evolved partly through cross-border influence and the adoption of shared regulatory principles across jurisdictions", "Every country's privacy law developed in complete isolation with no external influence", "Privacy law is identical worldwide, with zero variation", "International influence on domestic law is legally impossible"], 0, "Privacy law has evolved partly through cross-border influence and the adoption of shared regulatory principles across jurisdictions."),
  makeQuestion(25, "A security researcher discovers a critical vulnerability in a bank's app, privately notifies the bank, and gives them 90 days to fix it before any public disclosure. This practice is best described as:", ["Ethical (or \"white hat\") hacking following responsible disclosure norms", "Illegal and unethical black-hat hacking with no redeeming justification", "Corporate espionage", "Cyber-squatting"], 0, "Ethical (or \"white hat\") hacking following responsible disclosure norms."),
  makeQuestion(26, "A self-described \"hacktivist\" breaks into a corporation's systems and leaks internal documents to expose alleged wrongdoing, without the corporation's consent and without following any responsible disclosure process. How is this generally evaluated from an ethics-of-hacking perspective?", ["It raises significant ethical tension between the stated public-interest motive and the unauthorized, potentially harmful means used to achieve it", "It is automatically and uncontroversially ethical because the motive was to expose wrongdoing", "It carries no ethical complexity whatsoever", "It is identical, ethically, to a company-authorized penetration test"], 0, "It raises significant ethical tension between the stated public-interest motive and the unauthorized, potentially harmful means used to achieve it."),
  makeQuestion(27, "A company hires a certified penetration tester under a signed agreement (a \"scope of work\") to attempt breaking into its systems and report vulnerabilities. This activity is best classified as:", ["Authorized, ethical security testing distinct from unauthorized hacking", "Illegal hacking regardless of the agreement, since any intrusion is inherently criminal", "A privacy violation of the company's own systems", "A copyright violation"], 0, "Authorized, ethical security testing distinct from unauthorized hacking."),
  makeQuestion(28, "A former employee, angry about being fired, uses credentials that were never revoked to access and delete company files after his termination. This is best classified as:", ["Unauthorized access and likely a criminal cybercrime offense, regardless of his prior legitimate employment", "Legal, because he once had legitimate authorized access", "A protected whistleblowing action", "A copyright dispute only"], 0, "Unauthorized access and likely a criminal cybercrime offense, regardless of his prior legitimate employment."),
  makeQuestion(29, "A researcher argues that some hacking, even when technically unauthorized, can be ethically justified if it exposes serious wrongdoing with minimal harm and follows careful, limited disclosure. Which ethical framework does this argument most resemble?", ["A consequentialist (outcome-based) justification, weighing overall benefits against harms and least-harmful means", "An absolute rule that all unauthorized access is always ethical with no conditions", "An argument that ethics never applies to hacking under any circumstances", "A purely legal, non-ethical argument"], 0, "A consequentialist (outcome-based) justification, weighing overall benefits against harms and least-harmful means."),
  makeQuestion(30, "A \"gray hat\" hacker breaks into a system without permission, finds a vulnerability, but only reports it to the company afterward instead of exploiting it maliciously. How does this differ ethically from clear black-hat hacking?", ["The lack of malicious intent and subsequent disclosure mitigate but do not fully eliminate the ethical and legal concerns raised by unauthorized access", "It is identical to black-hat hacking in every ethical respect", "It is identical to fully authorized white-hat testing in every respect", "There is no ethical distinction among hacker categories at all"], 0, "The lack of malicious intent and subsequent disclosure mitigate but do not fully eliminate the ethical and legal concerns raised by unauthorized access."),
  makeQuestion(31, "A company publicly runs a \"bug bounty\" program, explicitly inviting security researchers to test its systems within defined rules and offering rewards for valid findings. This structure exists primarily to:", ["Create a lawful, ethical, and mutually beneficial channel for vulnerability discovery that avoids the ambiguity of unauthorized hacking", "Encourage illegal hacking by outsiders", "Eliminate the company's own responsibility for security entirely", "Replace the need for any internal security team permanently"], 0, "Create a lawful, ethical, and mutually beneficial channel for vulnerability discovery that avoids the ambiguity of unauthorized hacking."),
  makeQuestion(32, "A state-sponsored group hacks into a rival nation's power grid control systems, potentially threatening civilian safety, under the justification of \"national security.\" How does the scale and potential harm affect the ethical evaluation compared to typical criminal hacking?", ["The scale of potential harm (to civilian infrastructure and safety) raises the ethical and humanitarian stakes considerably higher than typical criminal hacking", "There is no ethical difference regardless of target or potential harm", "State-sponsored hacking of infrastructure is automatically ethical because a government authorized it", "Ethical evaluation is irrelevant once national security is invoked"], 0, "The scale of potential harm (to civilian infrastructure and safety) raises the ethical and humanitarian stakes considerably higher than typical criminal hacking."),
  makeQuestion(33, "A cybersecurity firm publicly discloses a vulnerability in widely used software immediately upon discovery, without notifying the vendor first, causing attackers to exploit it before a patch exists. This illustrates the risk of:", ["Irresponsible disclosure, which can cause more harm than good compared to coordinated disclosure practices", "A fully ethical and risk-free approach with no downside", "A copyright violation only", "A trademark violation only"], 0, "Irresponsible disclosure, which can cause more harm than good compared to coordinated disclosure practices."),
  makeQuestion(34, "An ethical hacker discovers, during an authorized penetration test, evidence suggesting the client company is engaged in unrelated illegal activity outside the test's scope. What is the most appropriate response?", ["Report findings within the agreed scope, and separately consider appropriate ethical and legal channels for the unrelated discovery, rather than exceeding the authorized scope unilaterally", "Immediately exploit the unrelated finding to expose the company publicly, regardless of the agreed scope", "Ignore it completely and never mention it to anyone", "Use the discovery for personal financial gain"], 0, "Report findings within the agreed scope, and separately consider appropriate ethical and legal channels for the unrelated discovery, rather than exceeding the authorized scope unilaterally."),
  makeQuestion(35, "A privacy advocate argues that pervasive corporate data collection functions like a form of \"surveillance capitalism.\" What is the core concern behind this framing?", ["That businesses are increasingly monetizing detailed behavioral data in ways that may undermine individual autonomy and consent", "That all forms of business are inherently criminal", "That advertising has never existed before the internet era", "That the concern is purely about copyright, not data practices"], 0, "That businesses are increasingly monetizing detailed behavioral data in ways that may undermine individual autonomy and consent."),
  makeQuestion(36, "A smart home device manufacturer continuously records audio in users' homes and shares snippets with third-party contractors for \"quality improvement,\" buried in fine print few users read. This scenario most directly raises which combined issue?", ["Both the value of privacy and the ethics of meaningful, informed consent", "Only a copyright issue", "Only a trademark issue", "Only a labor law issue"], 0, "Both the value of privacy and the ethics of meaningful, informed consent."),
  makeQuestion(37, "A country's supreme court rules that mass warrantless government data collection violates constitutional privacy protections, even though the collection was for stated security purposes. This ruling reflects which broader principle?", ["That privacy protections can act as a constitutional check on government power, even when security justifications are invoked", "That security concerns always automatically override any privacy protection", "That courts have no legitimate role in privacy matters", "That constitutional protections never apply to government surveillance"], 0, "That privacy protections can act as a constitutional check on government power, even when security justifications are invoked."),
  makeQuestion(38, "An ethical hacker is offered a large sum by an unknown third party to sell a critical vulnerability instead of reporting it to the affected vendor. Selling it instead of disclosing it responsibly primarily raises concerns about:", ["Prioritizing personal profit over preventing foreseeable harm to potentially millions of affected users", "No ethical concern at all, since selling information is always acceptable", "A copyright violation", "A trademark violation"], 0, "Prioritizing personal profit over preventing foreseeable harm to potentially millions of affected users."),
  makeQuestion(39, "Which statement best summarizes the relationship between privacy and security in cybersecurity ethics?", ["They are related but distinct: privacy concerns controlling personal information, while security concerns protecting systems and data from unauthorized access or harm, and strong practice often requires balancing both", "They are identical concepts with no meaningful distinction", "Security concerns always override privacy with no need for balance", "Privacy and security are entirely unrelated fields with no overlap"], 0, "They are related but distinct: privacy concerns controlling personal information, while security concerns protecting systems and data from unauthorized access or harm, and strong practice often requires balancing both."),
  makeQuestion(40, "A university publishes students' grades by full name and student number on a public hallway bulletin board, arguing that \"grades aren't secret information.\" A student objects. Whose position is more consistent with modern privacy norms?", ["The student's; academic records are generally treated as sensitive personal information requiring consent-based, limited disclosure", "The university's; grades can never be considered private information", "Neither; privacy norms have no relevance to educational records", "The university's, as long as the board is taken down after one week"], 0, "The student's; academic records are generally treated as sensitive personal information requiring consent-based, limited disclosure."),
];

// Chapter 6: Digital Intellectual Property (IP) (25 questions)
const chapter6Questions: QuizQuestion[] = [
  makeQuestion(1, "A startup founder insists \"intellectual property\" only refers to physical inventions like machines. Is this an accurate understanding of the term?", ["No; intellectual property broadly covers creations of the mind, including inventions, literary and artistic works, designs, symbols, names, and software", "Yes, IP applies only to tangible physical machines", "Yes, but only for inventions patented before 2000", "No, IP applies only to trademarks, never inventions"], 0, "No; intellectual property broadly covers creations of the mind, including inventions, literary and artistic works, designs, symbols, names, and software."),
  makeQuestion(2, "A mobile app combines original artwork (potentially copyrightable), a unique algorithm (potentially patentable), and a distinctive brand name (potentially trademarkable). This illustrates that:", ["A single digital product can involve multiple distinct categories of intellectual property simultaneously", "Only one category of IP can ever apply to any single product", "Digital products cannot be protected by any form of IP", "Software is legally identical to patents in every respect"], 0, "A single digital product can involve multiple distinct categories of intellectual property simultaneously."),
  makeQuestion(3, "Which of the following is generally considered a core justification for granting intellectual property rights at all?", ["Incentivizing creativity and innovation by allowing creators to benefit economically from their work", "Ensuring that all information is always kept permanently secret", "Preventing any sharing of ideas under any circumstances", "Guaranteeing government ownership of all inventions"], 0, "Incentivizing creativity and innovation by allowing creators to benefit economically from their work."),
  makeQuestion(4, "A small developer argues software should never be protected by any IP law because \"code is just instructions, not real property.\" How do most legal systems, including the Philippines, generally treat original software code?", ["As a copyrightable literary work, among other possible protections such as patents for certain technical processes", "As entirely unprotectable under any circumstances", "As protected only by trademark law", "As protected only under labor law"], 0, "As a copyrightable literary work, among other possible protections such as patents for certain technical processes."),
  makeQuestion(5, "Digital intellectual property (e.g., software, digital art, e-books) differs from traditional physical property in which key respect relevant to IP law?", ["Digital works can typically be copied perfectly and distributed at near-zero marginal cost, raising unique enforcement challenges", "Digital works cannot be copied at all under any circumstances", "Digital works are automatically exempt from all IP law", "Digital works are always more expensive to produce than physical goods"], 0, "Digital works can typically be copied perfectly and distributed at near-zero marginal cost, raising unique enforcement challenges."),
  makeQuestion(6, "A philosopher argues that a creator has a natural moral right to control and benefit from what they have created through their own labor and intellect. This reasoning most closely reflects which philosophical justification for IP?", ["A labor-desert (Lockean) theory of property rights", "A purely utilitarian, incentive-based theory only", "A theory that IP should never exist", "A theory based solely on government convenience"], 0, "A labor-desert (Lockean) theory of property rights."),
  makeQuestion(7, "Another common justification for IP argues that granting temporary exclusive rights ultimately benefits society by encouraging more innovation than would occur without such incentives, even though it limits free copying in the short term. This reflects which type of justification?", ["A utilitarian/consequentialist justification, balancing individual incentives against overall social benefit", "A justification based purely on natural moral rights with no social benefit component", "An argument that IP should never expire", "An argument rejecting all forms of exclusivity"], 0, "A utilitarian/consequentialist justification, balancing individual incentives against overall social benefit."),
  makeQuestion(8, "A critic of strong IP protections argues that treating ideas as \"property\" is philosophically different from owning physical objects, since ideas can be used by many people simultaneously without depriving others. This argument most directly challenges:", ["The straightforward application of traditional physical-property reasoning to intangible intellectual works", "The existence of copyright law in any form whatsoever", "The idea that trademarks should exist", "The idea that patents ever expire"], 0, "The straightforward application of traditional physical-property reasoning to intangible intellectual works."),
  makeQuestion(9, "A software patent is granted for a genuinely novel and non-obvious technical process, giving the inventor exclusive rights for a limited period. What is the primary philosophical trade-off being made by society in granting this patent?", ["Temporarily limiting others' freedom to use the innovation, in exchange for encouraging disclosure and future innovation", "No trade-off exists; patents impose no cost on society whatsoever", "Permanently and irrevocably removing the idea from all future use, with no expiration", "Patents exist purely to benefit government revenue"], 0, "Temporarily limiting others' freedom to use the innovation, in exchange for encouraging disclosure and future innovation."),
  makeQuestion(10, "Which scenario best illustrates the tension between rewarding individual creators and enabling broader societal access to knowledge that underlies IP philosophy debates?", ["A life-saving software algorithm is patented, delaying free access by competitors for a period, in exchange for the developer recouping R&D investment and disclosure of the method", "A public domain work is used freely by anyone with no restriction", "A trademark is renewed indefinitely with proper use", "A copyright automatically transfers between owners without any agreement"], 0, "A life-saving software algorithm is patented, delaying free access by competitors for a period, in exchange for the developer recouping R&D investment and disclosure of the method."),
  makeQuestion(11, "A company distributes its software under a proprietary license that keeps the source code secret and prohibits users from modifying, redistributing, or reverse-engineering it. This is best classified as:", ["Proprietary (closed-source) software licensing", "Free and Open-Source Software (FOSS) licensing", "Public domain software with no license at all", "Freeware with full source code access"], 0, "Proprietary (closed-source) software licensing."),
  makeQuestion(12, "A nonprofit chooses Free and Open-Source Software (FOSS) for its core infrastructure specifically because it wants the ability to inspect, modify, and redistribute the source code as needed. Which factor most directly enables this?", ["FOSS licenses grant users the freedom to access and modify source code, unlike typical proprietary licenses", "FOSS is always completely free of any licensing terms or restrictions", "FOSS software is technically simpler than proprietary software in every case", "Proprietary software always includes full source-code access"], 0, "FOSS licenses grant users the freedom to access and modify source code, unlike typical proprietary licenses."),
  makeQuestion(13, "A company builds its core commercial product on top of an open-source library licensed under a permissive license (e.g., MIT), which does not require sharing derivative source code. This is best described as:", ["A legitimate and common practice, as permissive open-source licenses generally allow such proprietary derivative use", "Automatically illegal under all open-source licenses without exception", "A copyright violation regardless of license terms", "Only allowed for non-commercial derivative works"], 0, "A legitimate and common practice, as permissive open-source licenses generally allow such proprietary derivative use."),
  makeQuestion(14, "A developer modifies and redistributes software licensed under a strict copyleft license (e.g., GPL) but refuses to release the modified source code, despite distributing the compiled program to others. This is most likely:", ["A violation of the copyleft license's core requirement to share derivative source code under the same terms", "Fully compliant, since compiled programs are exempt from copyleft obligations", "A trademark violation, not a licensing issue", "Irrelevant, since GPL software has no enforceable terms"], 0, "A violation of the copyleft license's core requirement to share derivative source code under the same terms."),
  makeQuestion(15, "A company argues that using proprietary software guarantees better security than open-source alternatives because \"the code is hidden from attackers.\" Which counterargument is commonly raised by open-source proponents?", ["Open peer review of source code can help identify and fix vulnerabilities faster, an argument sometimes summarized as \"given enough eyeballs, all bugs are shallow\"", "Hidden source code is always mathematically proven more secure in every case", "Open-source software cannot legally be used for security-critical applications", "Proprietary software is always free of any vulnerabilities"], 0, "Open peer review of source code can help identify and fix vulnerabilities faster, an argument sometimes summarized as \"given enough eyeballs, all bugs are shallow\"."),
  makeQuestion(16, "A government agency mandates that all newly developed public-sector software must be released under an open-source license whenever feasible, to promote transparency and reduce vendor lock-in. This policy primarily reflects which value associated with FOSS?", ["Transparency, interoperability, and reduced dependency on any single proprietary vendor", "A requirement that all software must always be free of charge", "A mandate that eliminates the need for any IP law", "A rule that only applies to private companies, not government"], 0, "Transparency, interoperability, and reduced dependency on any single proprietary vendor."),
  makeQuestion(17, "A developer wants to combine code from a strong copyleft-licensed project with code from a proprietary, closed-source project into a single distributed application. What is the most significant legal risk in this scenario?", ["Potential license incompatibility, since copyleft terms may require the entire combined work to be released under compatible open terms", "No risk at all, since licenses never conflict with each other", "The risk applies only if the software is sold, never if given away free", "Only trademark law is implicated, not licensing terms"], 0, "Potential license incompatibility, since copyleft terms may require the entire combined work to be released under compatible open terms."),
  makeQuestion(18, "A small business uses \"freeware\" video editing software with no cost, but the license explicitly prohibits any commercial use of the output. The business uses it to edit and sell client videos anyway. This is best classified as:", ["A breach of the freeware license's usage terms, despite the software itself being free of charge", "Fully compliant, since freeware has no enforceable terms of any kind", "A trademark violation only", "A patent violation only"], 0, "A breach of the freeware license's usage terms, despite the software itself being free of charge."),
  makeQuestion(19, "Comparing \"free software\" (in the FOSS sense of freedom) to \"free\" as in zero-cost, which distinction is most accurate?", ["FOSS's core meaning centers on user freedoms (to use, study, modify, and share), which is a separate concept from whether the software costs money", "The two terms are always identical in meaning and legal effect", "FOSS software must always be free of charge and cannot be sold under any license", "Freedom and zero-cost are legally interchangeable requirements"], 0, "FOSS's core meaning centers on user freedoms (to use, study, modify, and share), which is a separate concept from whether the software costs money."),
  makeQuestion(20, "A company that builds proprietary software on top of several FOSS components fails to comply with the attribution and license-disclosure requirements of those components' licenses. What is the most likely consequence?", ["Potential legal exposure for license violations, even though the underlying open-source code was freely available", "No consequence, since open-source code carries no enforceable obligations", "A criminal cybercrime charge exclusively", "A trademark cancellation only"], 0, "Potential legal exposure for license violations, even though the underlying open-source code was freely available."),
  makeQuestion(21, "A university researcher publishes an open-access dataset and algorithm under a permissive license specifically to accelerate collective scientific progress, forgoing potential exclusive commercial rights. Which IP philosophy does this choice best reflect?", ["A view that, in certain contexts, open sharing better serves innovation and societal benefit than exclusive proprietary control", "A view that all IP protection should be abolished in every context", "A view that patents are always more valuable than open sharing", "A view that has no connection to any IP philosophy at all"], 0, "A view that, in certain contexts, open sharing better serves innovation and societal benefit than exclusive proprietary control."),
  makeQuestion(22, "A company claims its proprietary software's exclusivity is the only way to ensure quality and continued investment, dismissing FOSS models as inherently unsustainable. What is a common counterexample cited by FOSS proponents?", ["Numerous widely used and well-maintained large-scale FOSS projects (e.g., major operating system kernels and web infrastructure) are sustained through community and corporate contributions", "There are no successful FOSS projects of significant scale in existence", "FOSS projects are legally prohibited from receiving corporate funding", "FOSS software is defined by having no formal maintenance process"], 0, "Numerous widely used and well-maintained large-scale FOSS projects (e.g., major operating system kernels and web infrastructure) are sustained through community and corporate contributions."),
  makeQuestion(23, "A developer wants to choose between releasing a new tool as fully proprietary, permissively licensed open-source, or strong copyleft open-source. Which factor most directly should guide this decision from an IP standpoint?", ["The developer's goals regarding control, monetization, and how derivative works may be used or shared by others", "The color scheme of the tool's user interface", "The number of employees at a competing company", "The developer's personal age"], 0, "The developer's goals regarding control, monetization, and how derivative works may be used or shared by others."),
  makeQuestion(24, "A company builds a business model entirely around providing paid support, customization, and hosting services for otherwise freely available open-source software. This illustrates that:", ["Value and revenue in a FOSS model can come from services and support rather than exclusive control over the underlying code itself", "FOSS software can never generate any revenue for any company", "This business model is illegal under open-source licenses", "Proprietary licensing is the only way to generate software revenue"], 0, "Value and revenue in a FOSS model can come from services and support rather than exclusive control over the underlying code itself."),
  makeQuestion(25, "A digital artist argues that traditional copyright frameworks, designed largely around physical and print-era works, sometimes struggle to address novel digital IP issues (like AI-generated art or NFTs). This observation illustrates:", ["That IP law and philosophy continue to evolve and face new challenges as digital technology advances", "That IP law has remained completely unchanged since its creation", "That digital art can never be protected by any law", "That NFTs automatically resolve all IP ownership questions"], 0, "That IP law and philosophy continue to evolve and face new challenges as digital technology advances."),
];

// Chapter 7: Government Agencies Responsible in the Implementation of the Philippines IT Programs (25 questions)
